course: myths
lesson: 13
title: Earth from space
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Mr Hughes taps the board, very sure: "The Great Wall of China is the ONLY man-made thing you can see from space!" It's in quiz books, it sounds grand, and it began before anyone had even been to space.
explain: Then people went up and looked. The Wall is long but narrow and the same colour as the land around it, and astronauts, even China's first astronaut Yang Liwei, said they couldn't pick it out with their eyes; city lights at night are far easier.
explain: Space myths grow from what things look like: astronauts float, so "there's no gravity up there"; it's warm in summer, so "we must be closer to the Sun"; the Sun crosses the sky, so "it goes round us".
explain: The real reasons are better: astronauts float because they and their station are falling round the Earth together, seasons come from the Earth's tilt, and the Sun seems to move because the Earth spins.
explain: The sharpest argument is often a question: if summer came from being close to the Sun, why is it summer in Australia at Christmas?
fact: The Great Wall of China is long but narrow and land-coloured; astronauts say it is practically impossible to see with bare eyes from orbit, while cities at night are easy to see.
fact: Gravity at the International Space Station is about 90% as strong as on the ground; astronauts float because they are falling around the Earth together with their station; anything falling freely acts weightless, so water even stops pouring from holes in a dropped cup.
fact: A person in space without a suit would not explode; they would lose consciousness in about 15 seconds and need rescue within a minute or two, so it is deadly but not an explosion.
fact: The Earth is not a perfect ball: it is about 43 kilometres wider across the equator than from pole to pole.
fact: Seasons come from the Earth's tilt: in summer your half leans towards the Sun, so sunlight hits more directly and days are longer; the Earth is closest to the Sun in early January.
fact: The hottest temperatures ever recorded are in deserts away from the equator, such as Death Valley; equator rainforests are hot but cloudy and rainy.
fact: The Sun's light is basically white; our air scatters blue light, so the Sun looks yellowish, and red at sunset; from space it looks white.
fact: The Moon is often visible in the daytime sky, depending on its phase.
fact: Space is almost empty, so sound cannot travel through it; space is silent, and the explosion noises in films are made up.
fact: The Earth goes round the Sun once a year and spins once a day, which makes the Sun seem to move across the sky; Copernicus and Galileo argued for this centuries ago.
fact: Some claims that sound like myths are true, like silent space, so check before you argue.
exercise: Mr Hughes says the Great Wall is the only man-made thing you can see from space. What did astronauts report? | It's practically impossible to see with bare eyes from orbit | It's the only thing they could see | They could see it from the Moon | It glows at night so it's easy to spot
exercise: Why is the Great Wall so hard to see from orbit? | It is narrow and the same colour as the land | It is hidden under clouds all year | It is painted a special invisible blue colour | It is shorter than most motorways
exercise: Josh says there's no gravity in space, so astronauts float. Why do they really float? | They and their station are falling around the Earth together | Gravity switches off above the clouds | The station is full of helium | Their suits are lighter than air
exercise: How strong is gravity at the Space Station compared with on the ground? | About 90% as strong | Zero, none at all up there | Twice as strong as on the ground | About 1% as strong, almost nothing
exercise: Elsie says you'd explode in space without a suit. What would really happen? | You'd lose consciousness in about 15 seconds, deadly but no explosion | You'd burst like a balloon instantly | You'd freeze solid in one second | Nothing, you could breathe for an hour
exercise: Rowan says the Earth is a perfect ball. What's true? | It is slightly wider at the equator than pole to pole | It is a perfect sphere like a marble | It is shaped like an egg standing up | It is completely flat at the poles, like a big pancake
exercise: Sienna says summer is hot because we're closer to the Sun. What causes the seasons? | The Earth's tilt | Our distance from the Sun | The Sun getting hotter in summer | Clouds blocking the Sun in winter
exercise: When in the year is the Earth closest to the Sun? | Early January | Late July | The first day of spring | Every Sunday
exercise: Callum says the equator is the hottest place on Earth. Where were the hottest temperatures recorded? | In deserts away from the equator, like Death Valley | In the steamy rainforests right on the equator itself | At the South Pole in summer | On top of the tallest mountains
exercise: Tilly draws the Sun yellow. What colour is its light really? | Basically white; our air makes it look yellowish | Bright yellow all the time, like a lemon or a banana | Orange all the way through, like a big burning fire | Blue, just like the sky that we see in the daytime
exercise: Finn says the Moon only comes out at night. What's true? | It's often visible in the daytime, depending on its phase | It hides behind the Sun all day | It only exists at night | It is visible in the daytime only once a year, at midsummer
exercise: Darcy says space is totally silent because there's no air. Is she right? | Yes, sound needs something to travel through | No, explosions in space are very loud, like in films | No, space is full of music from stars | Yes, but only on the Moon
exercise: Grandpa says the Sun goes round the Earth. What's true? | The Earth spins daily and goes round the Sun yearly | The Sun really goes round the Earth once every single day | Both stand still and the sky moves | The Moon pulls the Sun around us
exercise: Who argued centuries ago that the Earth goes round the Sun? | Copernicus and Galileo | Isaac Newton and Charles Darwin | Julius Caesar and Cleopatra | The Wright brothers
exercise: What is easier to see from space than the Great Wall? | City lights at night | A single house on its own | A person waving their arms | A garden fence round a field
test: "My teacher literally said the Great Wall is visible from space, he's a teacher so he's right innit" What's a wise move? | After class, say astronauts couldn't spot it and offer to look it up together | Shout "wrong!" in the middle of the lesson | Agree, because teachers are never wrong | Never mention it to anyone
test: Sienna's brother insists summer is from being closer to the Sun. Which question beats him best? | Why is it summer in Australia when it's winter here? | Why does the Sun look so yellow in all of our drawings? | How many planets are there? | Why do we have weekends?
test: You poke holes in a paper cup of water and drop it. The water stops pouring while it falls. What does this show? | Falling things feel weightless, like astronauts | Gravity switches off for cups | Water is lighter than air | The holes in the cup close up tight when they fall
test: In a space film, an asteroid explodes with a huge BOOM heard by the heroes in their ship far away. What's wrong? | Sound can't travel through nearly empty space | Nothing, explosions are loud everywhere in the universe | It should be a quiet pop | Only big asteroids are silent
test: What would happen to the seasons if the Earth had no tilt at all? | There would be almost no seasons | Every season would be summer everywhere | Seasons would be twice as strong | Winter would last all year everywhere
test: At school pick-up you see a pale Moon in the blue afternoon sky. Your little brother says, "That's impossible!" What do you say? | It's normal; the Moon is often up in daytime | It must be a balloon | It's a fake Moon put up there for astronauts to practise | It's the Sun in disguise
test: Grandpa winks: "The Sun goes round us, look, it moves across the sky!" What's a fun way to show him? | Spin a ball near a torch to show day and night | Point at the Sun and stare at it | Draw the Sun on the ceiling | Tell him old people can't learn anything new anyway
test: An astronaut photo shows the Sun looking white. Your friend says the photo's broken. What's the truth? | From space, without our air, the Sun really looks white | The camera turned it white by mistake | The Sun goes white once a year | The Sun is white only at night
test: A cartoon astronaut takes off his helmet in space and blows up like a balloon. What's the real danger? | No air: he'd pass out in seconds and need rescue fast | He would pop at once | He'd float away happily | The cold would freeze him in a second
test: Which is a fair way to describe Earth's shape on a globe? | Nearly a ball, slightly wider at the equator | An exact, perfect ball with no bumps on it at all | A flat disc, like a dinner plate, seen from above | A big cube with rounded-off corners, like a dice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cause of the seasons upon the Earth? | The inclination of the Earth's axis relative to its orbit | The variation in the Earth's distance from the Sun | The variation in the Sun's emission of heat | The interposition of the Moon between the Earth and the Sun
exam: Identify the principal reason for which astronauts aboard an orbiting station appear weightless. | They and the station are in continuous free fall about the Earth | Gravity is wholly absent at the altitude of the station | The station is maintained at reduced air pressure | Their garments are lighter than the air within the station
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the visibility of the Great Wall of China from orbit. | It is practically impossible to discern with the unaided eye | It is the sole artificial structure visible from orbit to the eye | It is plainly visible from the surface of the Moon | It is visible solely during the hours of darkness
exam: Ascertain the period of the year during which the Earth is nearest to the Sun. | Early January | Late July | The spring equinox | The autumn equinox
